data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/home/<USER:1>/.local/share/cs-campaign/output/summary.md\", \"content\": \"# Campaign csrclkey — \\\"greet, tested\\\" — summary\\n\\n## Outcome: campaign-met\\n\\nAll three mission criteria satisfied:\\n\\n1. `src/greet.py` defines `greet(name)` returning `f\\\"Hello, {name}!\\\"`.\\n2. `tests/test_greet.py` covers a normal name (\\\"Ada\\\" -\u003e \\\"Hello, Ada!\\\") and the\\n   empty string (\\\"\\\" -\u003e \\\"Hello, !\\\").\\n3. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exits 0 (2 tests, both pass).\\n\\n## How verified\\n\\n- dev (teammate) implemented the work on branch\\n  `cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>`, commit `fbcc9d2`\\n  (\\\"Add greet function with unit tests\\\"), and reported success.\\n- Orchestrator did not trust the report alone: fetched the branch\\n  (`cs-campaign-member fetch dev`), inspected `git show --stat` and the file\\n  contents directly, then extracted the exact commit tree via\\n  `git archive fbcc9d2` into a clean scratch directory and re-ran\\n  `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` independently. Exit code 0, 2 tests\\n  passed, matching dev's report.\\n- Dispatch d002 accepted (`cs-campaign-member accept dev`).\\n\\n## Notes\\n\\n- Orchestrator wrote no source code itself, per its brief (`orchestrator.md`:\\n  \\\"you run this campaign and judge it; you write no code yourself\\\"). Its own\\n  branch (`cs-sandbox/<ORCHESTRATOR:1>.<GROUP:1>`) carries no\\n  code diff from base — all functional work is on dev's branch, which is the\\n  accepted, verified deliverable.\\n- Task was completed in a single dispatch/reply cycle with no rework needed.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":2,"type":"content_block_delta"}

